    content: "Year-end closing in Switzerland: steps, deadlines and law | nullish"
---

{% aside type="tip" title="In short" %}
A GmbH or an AG, like any sole proprietorship or partnership with at least CHF 500,000 in revenue, closes each fiscal year with a balance sheet, an income statement and notes. They must be ready and signed within six months after the end of the year, and approved by the shareholders within the same six months.
{% /aside %}

## What the year-end closing is

At the end of each fiscal year, you sum up all your entries in the annual financial statements. Under [Art. 958 CO](https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/27/317_321_377/en#art_958), they're made of three parts:

- **Balance sheet**: what the company owns and owes on the closing date.
- **Income statement**: the revenue and expenses of the year, and the profit or loss.
- **Notes**: the accounting principles used, and the information the figures alone don't show.

You sign these statements and have them approved. Large companies that need an ordinary audit also add a cash flow statement and a management report ([Art. 961 CO](https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/27/317_321_377/en#art_961)). Together, this is called the annual report.

## Who has to prepare annual financial statements

Legal entities such as a GmbH, an AG or a cooperative have to prepare annual financial statements, and so do sole proprietorships and partnerships with at least CHF 500,000 in revenue in the last fiscal year. Below that, a record of income, expenses, and what you own and owe is enough ([Art. 957 CO](https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/27/317_321_377/en#art_957)).

## Deadlines

| What                                                        | When                                                                       | Law                                                                                                                                                      |
| ----------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Annual report prepared and signed                           | Within six months after the end of the fiscal year                         | [Art. 958 para. 3 CO](https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/27/317_321_377/en#art_958)                                                                      |
| Approval by the general meeting (AG) or shareholders (GmbH) | Within six months after the end of the fiscal year                         | [Art. 699](https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/27/317_321_377/en#art_699) and [Art. 805 CO](https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/27/317_321_377/en#art_805) |
| VAT errors corrected                                        | At the latest in the VAT period that includes the 180th day after year-end | [Art. 72 VAT Act](https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/2009/615/en#art_72)                                                                                 |
| Tax return                                                  | Set by your canton                                                         |                                                                                                                                                          |
| Keeping the records                                         | Ten years, from the end of the fiscal year                                 | [Art. 958f CO](https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/27/317_321_377/en#art_958_f)                                                                           |

Example: your 2026 fiscal year runs from 1 January to 31 December 2026. Then:

- Annual report and meeting: by **30 June 2027**.
- VAT errors corrected: at the latest in the declaration that includes **29 June 2027**. That's the declaration for Q2 2027 (effective method) or for the first half of 2027 (net tax rate).
- Records kept: until the **end of 2036**.

The annual report is signed by the chair of the board, or of the management, and by the person in charge of the accounting.

## Step by step

{% steps %}

1. **Book everything.** All invoices sent and received in the year are recorded, each entry with its voucher.

2. **Reconcile your bank accounts.** The balance on the closing date must match your bank statement, to the cent.

3. **Check receivables and payables.** Is every open invoice still owed? Write off what won't be paid.

4. **Count your stock.** If you hold goods or materials, count them on the closing date and value them at most at their cost.

5. **Reconcile salaries.** The salary accounts must match what you declared to the AHV compensation office and the pension fund. Every employee gets a salary statement for the year.

6. **Accrue.** Revenue and expenses belong to the year they relate to ([Art. 958b CO](https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/27/317_321_377/en#art_958_b)). A December electricity bill that arrives in January is still an expense of the old year. If neither your net revenue nor your financial income exceeds CHF 100,000, you may book them when paid instead.

7. **Depreciate.** Assets are valued at most at their cost, and depreciation follows generally accepted commercial principles ([Art. 960a CO](https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/27/317_321_377/en#art_960_a)).

8. **Book provisions.** When past events let you expect an outflow in future years, book a provision for the amount likely needed ([Art. 960e CO](https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/27/317_321_377/en#art_960_e)). The most common one: the taxes on the year's profit, paid the year after.

9. **Reconcile VAT.** Compare your VAT declarations with the accounts. Any difference is corrected with the annual reconciliation form of the tax administration.

10. **Prepare the annual report.** Balance sheet, income statement and notes, then sign them.

11. **Audit, or opt out.** An ordinary audit is required when two of these are exceeded in two consecutive years: CHF 20 million balance sheet total, CHF 40 million revenue, 250 full-time positions ([Art. 727 CO](https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/27/317_321_377/en#art_727)). Otherwise a limited audit applies, which you can waive with the consent of all shareholders if you have no more than ten full-time positions ([Art. 727a CO](https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/27/317_321_377/en#art_727_a)).

12. **Approve and allocate the profit.** The shareholders approve the annual report and decide what happens to the profit. An AG or GmbH first allocates 5 % of it to the legal retained earnings, until these reach half of the share capital together with the legal capital reserve ([Art. 672 CO](https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/27/317_321_377/en#art_672)). For a holding company, the limit is 20 %.

13. **Close the year.** The result goes to equity, and the balances carry over to the next year.

{% /steps %}

## Example

A GmbH with CHF 20,000 of share capital, no loss carried forward and no reserves yet, closes 2026. Before the closing entries, its profit is CHF 48,400.

| Closing entry                             | Debit          | Credit              | CHF   |
| ----------------------------------------- | -------------- | ------------------- | ----- |
| Depreciation of the computers             | Depreciation   | IT equipment        | 3,000 |
| December electricity, invoiced in January | Energy expense | Accrued liabilities | 400   |
| Taxes on the 2026 profit                  | Direct taxes   | Tax provisions      | 5,000 |

The profit drops to CHF 40,000. At the shareholders' meeting, 5 % of it, CHF 2,000, goes to the legal retained earnings. The limit is half of the share capital, CHF 10,000, so the allocation continues in the next years.

| Profit allocation         | Debit         | Credit                  | CHF    |
| ------------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------- | ------ |
| Legal retained earnings   | Annual profit | Legal retained earnings | 2,000  |
| Remainder carried forward | Annual profit | Retained earnings       | 38,000 |

## With nullish

Most of the closing happens in nullish:

- [Reconcile](/en/banking/reconciliation/) your bank movements and check the balances.
- Book accruals, depreciation and provisions in the [journal](/en/accounting/journal/).
- Close your [VAT periods](/en/accounting/vat/#vat-periods) and prepare the declaration.
- Check the [balance sheet and income statement](/en/accounting/reports/).
- [Close the year](/en/accounting/periods/#close-a-year): nullish books the annual result and the opening balances of the next year.
- [Export](/en/organization/#export-your-data) the organization's data and keep the copy for ten years.

The notes and the signatures stay with you.

## FAQ

### Can my fiscal year differ from the calendar year?

Yes. All deadlines then run from the end of your fiscal year. Found your GmbH in the autumn, for example, and your first fiscal year can run until the end of the next year. In nullish, set the start and end dates of the [period](/en/accounting/periods/) freely.

### Do I need a fiduciary?

The law doesn't require one. It requires a signed annual report, and an audit unless you opted out. Many companies still have an accountant prepare or review it.

### Does my GmbH need an auditor?

By default, a limited audit applies. With no more than ten full-time positions and the consent of all shareholders, you can opt out.

### How long do I keep everything?

Ten years, from the end of the fiscal year. The annual report and the audit report are kept printed and signed.

### What's the difference with the tax return?

The annual financial statements are the basis of the tax return. Its deadline depends on your canton.

{% aside type="note" %}
This guide summarizes the Swiss Code of Obligations and the VAT Act. It isn't legal or tax advice: for your own case, check with your accountant.
{% /aside %}
